use std
into aap.amni
lang amni
Amni-Drive's Android Auto Protocol (AAP) wire framing, multi-frame reassembly, and protobuf wire-format codec.
Constants: `FLAG_FIRST` is 1, `FLAG_LAST` is 2, `FLAG_SINGLE` is 3, `FLAG_ENCRYPTED` is 8, `FLAG_CONTROL` is 4, `MAX_FRAME_PAYLOAD` is 16384.
Struct `FrameHeader` holds `channel` and `flags` as unsigned 8-bit integers, `length` as an unsigned 16-bit integer, `total_length` as an unsigned 32-bit integer, and `header_size` as a 32-bit integer.
Struct `Reassembler` holds `active` as a boolean, `channel` and `flags` as unsigned 8-bit integers, `expected_total` as an unsigned 32-bit integer, and `buf` as a growable byte buffer.
Struct `PbField` holds `field_num` and `wire_type` as unsigned 32-bit integers, `varint` as an unsigned 64-bit integer, `bytes_off` and `bytes_len` as 32-bit integers, and `next_off` as a 32-bit integer.
Function `encode_frame_header` takes `channel` and `flags` as unsigned 8-bit integers, `payload_len` as an unsigned 16-bit integer, `total_len` as an unsigned 32-bit integer, and `out` as a mutable growable byte buffer. Append `channel`, then `flags`, then `payload_len` as 2 big-endian bytes. If `flags` has `FLAG_FIRST` set and does not have `FLAG_LAST` set, also append `total_len` as 4 big-endian bytes.
Function `decode_frame_header` takes `data` as a read-only byte slice and returns a `FrameHeader`. If `data` has fewer than 4 bytes, return a zeroed `FrameHeader` with `header_size` 0. Read `channel = data[0]`, `flags = data[1]`, and big-endian 16-bit `length` from `data[2..4]`. When `flags` has `FLAG_FIRST` set and not `FLAG_LAST`, require at least 8 bytes (otherwise return `header_size` 0), read big-endian 32-bit `total_length` from `data[4..8]`, and set `header_size` to 8. Otherwise set `total_length` to `length` and `header_size` to 4.
Function `reassemble_push` takes a mutable `Reassembler` `r`, `hdr` as `FrameHeader`, `payload` as a read-only byte slice, and `completed` as a mutable growable byte buffer. Clear `completed`. If `hdr.flags` has both `FLAG_FIRST` and `FLAG_LAST` set (single frame), copy `payload` into `completed`, set `r.active` false, and return true. If `hdr.flags` has `FLAG_FIRST` set (start of multi-frame), clear `r.buf`, append `payload`, set `r.active` true, `r.channel = hdr.channel`, `r.flags = hdr.flags`, `r.expected_total = hdr.total_length`, and return false. Otherwise if `r.active` is true and `r.channel` equals `hdr.channel`, append `payload` to `r.buf`; if `hdr.flags` has `FLAG_LAST` set or `r.buf` length is at least `r.expected_total`, copy `r.buf` into `completed`, clear `r.buf`, set `r.active` false, and return true. Otherwise return false.
Function `pb_put_varint` takes `val` as an unsigned 64-bit integer and `out` as a mutable growable byte buffer, and appends standard protobuf base-128 varint bytes.
Function `pb_put_tag` takes `field_num` and `wire_type` as unsigned 32-bit integers and `out` as a mutable growable byte buffer, and appends `pb_put_varint` of `(field_num << 3) | (wire_type & 7)`.
Function `pb_put_bytes` takes `field_num` as an unsigned 32-bit integer, `bytes` as a read-only byte slice, and `out` as a mutable growable byte buffer; writes tag with wire type 2, then varint length, then copies `bytes`.
Function `pb_next_field` takes `data` as a read-only byte slice and `offset` as a 32-bit integer, returning a `PbField`. If `offset` is negative or at least the length of `data`, return a zeroed `PbField` with `next_off` equal to -1. Decode a base-128 varint tag starting at `offset` (up to 10 bytes); split `field_num = tag >> 3` and `wire_type = tag & 7`. For wire type 0, decode a varint into `varint`. For wire type 1, read 8 little-endian bytes into `varint`. For wire type 5, read 4 little-endian bytes into `varint`. For wire type 2, decode length varint, set `bytes_off` and `bytes_len`, and advance past the slice. If any read overflows `data`, return `next_off` equal to -1.
